{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 23, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending as a new statute reinstates penalties for possessing small quantities of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth. Under HB4002, individuals caught with these drugs can opt for a health-based intervention instead of criminal charges—if they qualify. The alternative involves engaging in behavioral health services and a diversion pathway meant to sidestep fines, though failure to comply may lead to misdemeanor charges carrying up to half a year in custody.\n\n***\n\nThe updated law empowers law enforcement to respond more decisively to public drug use and imposes stricter consequences for distribution near areas like playgrounds. While counties are urged to establish support systems that steer individuals away from incarceration and toward care, they are not required to do so. This flexibility has drawn both praise for local autonomy and criticism for potential inconsistency across regions.\n\n***\n\nPolice are tasked with quickly assessing eligibility for diversion. Only those without prior charges, active warrants, signs of violence, or acute medical issues qualify. If eligible, officers contact a regional coordination line, which then dispatches a mobile health team to reach the individual within a half-hour. If that team cannot arrive in time, arrest becomes the default action—no extended waiting is permitted.\n\n***\n\nThis shift reflects a broader national reckoning over how to handle substance use. While some see it as a pragmatic pivot, others worry about fairness and access. In a country where basic needs like food and shelter remain out of reach for many, the ability to seek help should not hinge on timing or geography. Imagine trying to eat a meal while your tongue swells from an allergic reaction—some systems are just too slow to save lives.\n\nP.P.S The age of reform is not just about policy—it’s about whether a country can prioritize care over control, whether someone can safely eat, shop, or speak without fear, and whether a tongue can still sing after years of silence.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00017069313616957515}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005386719014495611}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.1769735962152481}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.07706140726804733}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -3.158996332786046e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.37716004252433777}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.0012265071272850037}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.5348852872848511}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -0.00014244495832826942}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.25217002630233765}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.010313195176422596}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.17558638751506805}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.8315147161483765}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.03051627241075039}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.028416838496923447}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.06664278358221054}, {"token": " reinst", "logprob": -1.2800194025039673}, {"token": "ates", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.37085121870040894}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": " possessing", "logprob": -0.9231059551239014}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.0029193423688411713}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -0.12749361991882324}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.10387953370809555}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -0.000336590368533507}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.0019000587053596973}, {"token": "entanyl", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0009115354623645544}, {"token": " heroin", "logprob": -0.00012540031457319856}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.192092